54-5f2c-9fc6-e3011ac4050b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192615 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2576" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192615 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the measure of the angles shown below.\n\n![](images/8a/8a74f14be370f7ca987cd5271572477f4e15a91f54be9ad662ec1455af7c4183.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192615 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "55e26a5c-18da-5923-a45f-f5cdaed73ac2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192615 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use a protractor to determine the measure of the angles.\n\nTo do this, we align one side of an angle with the zero mark (on the left or on the right side of the protractor) and read the degrees where the second side of the angle intersects the scale of numbers:\n\nBy doing this, we get the following angle measures.\n\n![](images/0b/0bf274dff799a9b7695c0e8dfc5f3850ac9c196f94d3ffb0210fc1df1bf12386.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192616 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2589" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192616 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the measure of the angle $\\angle ABC$ shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/feb2c7d5645b1443194baabcf4f7153fe15cb8059e4f590e2ee30a3a80a29aef.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192616 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "76f4a24f-4d7b-5be8-a86a-b1ae86c9f991" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192616 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the angle, we can use either scale of the protractor.\n\n- First, let's use the upper scale. The side $\\overrightarrow{BA}$ of the angle intersects the upper scale at $125^\\circ$, and the other side $\\overrightarrow{BC}$ intersects the upper scale at $20^\\circ$. To find the measure of $\\angle ABC$, we subtract the smaller reading from the larger one and get\n$m\\angle ABC = 125^\\circ - 20^\\circ = 105^\\circ$.\n- If we use the lower scale, we get the same result. The side $\\overrightarrow{BA}$ of the angle intersects the lower scale at $55^\\circ$, and the other side $\\overrightarrow{BC}$ intersects the lower scale at $160^\\circ$. Subtracting the smaller reading from the larger one, we get\n$m\\angle ABC = 160^\\circ - 55^\\circ = 105^\\circ$.\n\nWe can check our result by rotating the protractor to align its zero with the side $\\overrightarrow{BC}$, as shown below. This way, we can perform the usual process, taking only a single measurement. Indeed, we see that $m\\angle ABC$ is equal to $105^\\circ$.\n\n![](images/02/0292b94168d618a8fe48b47c524b71b5b3ca40fa42c89f104b2b8eca10f3a847.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192856 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192856 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192856 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192856 #edb/ref 1039 "Measuring Angles Using a Protractor" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192856 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186192615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192856 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4de6c0f2-b9c3-518d-ba86-40ec3fcbd4f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192857 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192857 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192857 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192857 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding an Angle Measure by Taking a Difference" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192857 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186192616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192857 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e42452f6-bf63-5c2e-a4d0-4ed5f6124c91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192891 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192891 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192891 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192891 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192891 #edb/ref 1045 "Measuring Angles Using a Protractor" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192891 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186193791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192891 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192891 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "8fdd2a28-fb77-5389-95e2-b00162ba12d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193326 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186191883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193326 #edb/ref 1069 "q-40668" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193326 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/fb/fb3c9b1a249cfd2b6f13e2e7b3a731549094b7e6ea38432d5dcd000a7083f7ec.png)\n\nDetermine the measure of the angle $∠ABC$ shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193326 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fa2e299b-8053-575c-a445-dc3a8b58a009" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193345 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186191997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193345 #edb/ref 1069 "q-52640" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193345 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cc/ccda9a0334c7981def5e4a3f96cc6a22c9e71cc1218b8e1b6dc8084f08abbffa.png)\n\nDetermine the measure of the angle $∠ABC$ shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193345 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ef364631-7848-5586-9790-7f94e2b665da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193790 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193790 #edb/ref 1061 8747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193790 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bce5c56d-9855-51ed-a0e7-6211798ef991" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193790 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193791 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193791 #edb/ref 1061 8751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193791 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7ea36da5-bcd8-5e22-ad3a-6ec6864a217a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193791 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193792 #edb/ref 1061 8771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193792 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "05f0b3a4-5dcf-5d3c-b6ea-cfcafe8b8203" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193792 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193813 #edb/ref 1064 "We use a **protractor** to measure angles. A protractor is an instrument that looks as follows:\n\n![](images/2c/2c8865e67ff36fe06a6d1cf1be8c591468f1fef98b060ccb404b3bb61509edcf.png)\n\nTo find the measure of an angle using a protractor, we follow three steps:\n\n1. Place the midpoint of the protractor on the vertex of the angle.\n2. Align one side of the angle with the line passing through the midpoint and zero on the protractor.\n3. Read off the degrees where the other side of the angle intersects the scale.\n\nLet's measure the following angle using a protractor.\n\n![](images/ba/baebe10676f4e321f0e3983ffd848718f0a569a601f7e7fef36679f05984dd08.png)\n\nWe place the midpoint of our protractor on the vertex and align a zero on the protractor with one of the sides.\n\n![](images/5f/5f4de191857fa5b08e9b1675205149108ce0db0e46815a572cccddb665865f69.png)\n\nReading the protractor, we see that the angle measures $45^\\circ$.\n\n**Warning:** Protractors usually have two scales in opposite directions. Be careful which one you use. In this case, we used the *upper* scale because this is the one that starts from zero as we measure counterclockwise." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193813 #edb/ref 1065 2181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193813 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7445a02a-ecba-53d9-88d7-765a590a504d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193813 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
